# Expectation record for 07_variadics/006_many_args_stack_spill.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 006_many_args_stack_spill
area               = 07_variadics
description        = Argument counts that exhaust every target's argument registers and force stack spill
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Each group passes far more arguments of one class than any ABI under test documents
argument registers for, so the paths those ABIs use once their registers are exhausted are
exercised.  Every va_arg names exactly the type supplied, no list is read past its argument
count, and each is ended exactly once.  All sums stay inside the range of their type and
every floating value is exactly representable.  One line is printed per retrieved argument,
so a single divergent line identifies the exact argument mishandled.  <stdarg.h> is included
because a variadic function cannot be written without it and it is in both compilers'
freestanding sets; printf is still hand-declared, because bcc ships no stdio.h.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
